      pgraft: {
        'User Guide': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Complete Tutorial',
              content: `**Step-by-step pgraft tutorial for setting up a production cluster.**

**Prerequisites:**
- 3 PostgreSQL servers (nodes)
- Network connectivity between nodes
- PostgreSQL 17+ installed on all nodes
- Root/sudo access for installation

**Step 1: Install pgraft on all nodes**
\`\`\`bash
# On each node
git clone https://github.com/pgElephant/pgraft.git
cd pgraft
make && sudo make install
\`\`\`

**Step 2: Configure PostgreSQL**
\`\`\`conf
# postgresql.conf on all nodes
shared_preload_libraries = 'pgraft'
wal_level = replica
max_wal_senders = 10
max_replication_slots = 10
hot_standby = on
\`\`\`

**Step 3: Initialize the cluster**
\`\`\`sql
-- On node1 (will become leader)
\\c postgres
CREATE EXTENSION pgraft;
SELECT pgraft_init();
\`\`\`

**Step 4: Add nodes to cluster**
\`\`\`sql
-- From the leader (node1)
SELECT pgraft_add_node(2, '192.168.1.102', 7002);
SELECT pgraft_add_node(3, '192.168.1.103', 7003);

-- Verify cluster formation
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_cluster_status();
\`\`\`

**Step 5: Test failover**
\`\`\`sql
-- Check current leader
SELECT pgraft_is_leader();

-- Simulate leader failure by stopping PostgreSQL on node1
-- Watch automatic failover to node2 or node3
\`\`\`

**Step 6: Verify cluster health**
\`\`\`sql
-- Check cluster status
SELECT * FROM pgraft_cluster_status();

-- Check node health
SELECT * FROM pgraft_node_health();

-- Monitor consensus metrics
SELECT * FROM pgraft_consensus_metrics();
\`\`\``
            },
            {
              title: 'Configuration Reference',
              content: `**Complete configuration reference for pgraft.**

**PostgreSQL Configuration:**
\`\`\`conf
# postgresql.conf
shared_preload_libraries = 'pgraft'

# WAL configuration for replication
wal_level = replica
max_wal_senders = 10
max_replication_slots = 10
hot_standby = on

# Memory settings
shared_buffers = 256MB
work_mem = 4MB
maintenance_work_mem = 64MB

# Connection settings
max_connections = 100
\`\`\`

**pgraft Configuration Parameters:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Consensus parameters
pgraft.heartbeat_interval = '100ms'        -- Heartbeat frequency
pgraft.election_timeout = '1000ms'         -- Election timeout
pgraft.max_log_entries = 10000            -- Max log entries before snapshot

-- Network settings
pgraft.listen_addr = '0.0.0.0:7001'       -- Listen address
pgraft.data_dir = '/var/lib/pgraft'       -- Data directory

-- Performance tuning
pgraft.batch_size = 100                   -- Replication batch size
pgraft.pipeline_size = 10                 -- Pipeline size
pgraft.compression_enabled = on           -- Enable log compression
\`\`\`

**Environment Variables:**
\`\`\`bash
export PGRAFT_CLUSTER_ID="my-cluster"
export PGRAFT_NODE_ID="node1"
export PGRAFT_LISTEN_ADDR="0.0.0.0:7001"
export PGRAFT_DATA_DIR="/var/lib/pgraft"
export PGRAFT_LOG_LEVEL="info"
\`\`\`

**Configuration Validation:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Check current configuration
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_config();

-- Test configuration
SELECT pgraft_test_config();

-- Reload configuration
SELECT pg_reload_conf();
\`\`\``
            },
            {
              title: 'SQL Functions',
              content: `**Complete SQL function reference for pgraft.**

**Cluster Management Functions:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Initialize a new node
SELECT pgraft_init();

-- Check if current node is leader
SELECT pgraft_is_leader();

-- Get cluster status
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_cluster_status();

-- Get cluster information
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_cluster_info();
\`\`\`

**Node Management Functions:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Add a new node to the cluster (only on leader)
SELECT pgraft_add_node(2, '127.0.0.1', 7002);
SELECT pgraft_add_node(3, '127.0.0.1', 7003);

-- Remove a node from the cluster
SELECT pgraft_remove_node('node2');

-- Get node health information
SELECT * FROM pgraft_node_health();

-- Get node statistics
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_node_stats('node1');
\`\`\`

**Leadership Functions:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Force a leader election (use with caution)
SELECT pgraft_force_election();

-- Get current leader information
SELECT pgraft_get_leader();

-- Check leadership status
SELECT pgraft_is_leader();
\`\`\`

**Monitoring Functions:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Get consensus metrics
SELECT * FROM pgraft_consensus_metrics();

-- Get log information
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_log_info();

-- Get log entries
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_log_entries(100, 200);

-- Get performance statistics
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_performance_stats();
\`\`\`

**Administrative Functions:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Pause consensus (for maintenance)
SELECT pgraft_pause_consensus();

-- Resume consensus
SELECT pgraft_resume_consensus();

-- Trigger manual snapshot
SELECT pgraft_trigger_snapshot();

-- Get version information
SELECT pgraft_version();
\`\`\``
            },
            {
              title: 'Cluster Operations',
              content: `**Operations guide for managing pgraft clusters.**

**Starting a Cluster:**
\`\`\`sql
-- 1. Initialize the first node (becomes leader)
SELECT pgraft_init();

-- 2. Add additional nodes
SELECT pgraft_add_node(2, 'node2.example.com', 7002);
SELECT pgraft_add_node(3, 'node3.example.com', 7003);

-- 3. Verify cluster formation
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_cluster_status();
\`\`\`

**Adding Nodes:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Always add nodes from the current leader
SELECT pgraft_is_leader(); -- Verify you're on leader

-- Add new node
SELECT pgraft_add_node(4, 'node4.example.com', 7002);

-- Verify node was added
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_cluster_info();
\`\`\`

**Removing Nodes:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Remove a node (from leader)
SELECT pgraft_remove_node('node4');

-- Verify node was removed
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_cluster_status();
\`\`\`

**Manual Failover:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Check current leader
SELECT pgraft_get_leader();

-- Force failover to specific node
SELECT pgraft_force_election();

-- Monitor failover progress
SELECT * FROM pgraft_consensus_metrics();
\`\`\`

**Maintenance Operations:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Pause consensus for maintenance
SELECT pgraft_pause_consensus();

-- Perform maintenance operations
-- ...

-- Resume consensus
SELECT pgraft_resume_consensus();

-- Verify cluster health after maintenance
SELECT * FROM pgraft_cluster_status();
\`\`\`

**Backup Operations:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Trigger snapshot before backup
SELECT pgraft_trigger_snapshot();

-- Perform PostgreSQL backup
-- pg_dump, pg_basebackup, etc.

-- Verify cluster consistency
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_log_info();
\`\`\``
            }
          ]
        },
        'Core Concepts': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Architecture',
              content: `**Understanding pgraft's architecture and design principles.**

**System Architecture:**
pgraft implements the Raft consensus algorithm as a PostgreSQL extension, providing distributed consensus with strong consistency guarantees.

**Core Components:**
1. **PostgreSQL Background Worker (C)**: Drives Raft ticks every 100ms
2. **pgraft_go_tick() [C→Go]**: Bridge between C and Go components  
3. **raftNode.Tick() [etcd-io/raft]**: Core Raft implementation
4. **raftProcessingLoop() [Goroutine]**: Handles Ready() messages
5. **Persist → Send → Apply → Advance**: Raft state machine flow

**Data Flow Architecture:**
\`\`\`
PostgreSQL Background Worker (C)
    ↓ Every 100ms
pgraft_go_tick() [C→Go]
    ↓
raftNode.Tick() [etcd-io/raft]
    ↓
Ready() messages
    ↓
raftProcessingLoop() [Goroutine]
    ↓
Persist → Send → Apply → Advance
\`\`\`

**Key Design Decisions:**
- **Worker-Driven**: PostgreSQL background worker actively drives Raft ticks
- **Persistent Storage**: HardState, log entries, and snapshots survive crashes
- **Zero Configuration**: Minimal setup required for basic operation
- **Production Ready**: 0 compilation errors/warnings, PostgreSQL C coding standards compliant

**Integration with PostgreSQL:**
- Runs as a PostgreSQL extension, not external process
- Uses PostgreSQL's native storage and networking
- Integrates with PostgreSQL's background worker system
- Provides SQL interface for all operations`
            },
            {
              title: 'Automatic Replication',
              content: `**How pgraft handles automatic replication and consistency.**

**Replication Model:**
pgraft uses the Raft consensus algorithm to ensure consistent replication across all cluster nodes.

**Automatic Replication Features:**
- **Leader-Based Replication**: Only the leader accepts writes
- **Log Replication**: All changes are replicated through Raft log
- **Consistent State**: All nodes maintain identical state
- **Automatic Recovery**: Failed nodes automatically catch up

**Replication Process:**
\`\`\`sql
-- 1. Client writes to leader
INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('John', 'john@example.com');

-- 2. Leader replicates to followers via Raft
-- This happens automatically in the background

-- 3. All nodes maintain consistent state
SELECT * FROM users; -- Returns same data on all nodes
\`\`\`

**Replication Guarantees:**
- **Strong Consistency**: All reads see the latest committed writes
- **Durability**: All writes are persisted before acknowledgment
- **Ordering**: Writes are applied in the same order on all nodes
- **Atomicity**: All-or-nothing semantics for multi-statement operations

**Replication Monitoring:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Check replication lag
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_cluster_status();

-- Monitor consensus metrics
SELECT * FROM pgraft_consensus_metrics();

-- Check node health
SELECT * FROM pgraft_node_health();
\`\`\`

**Recovery Scenarios:**
- **Node Recovery**: Failed nodes automatically rejoin and catch up
- **Network Partition**: Split-brain protection ensures single leader
- **Log Compaction**: Automatic snapshots prevent log growth
- **State Reconciliation**: Conflicting states are resolved via Raft`
            },
            {
              title: 'Split-Brain Protection',
              content: `**How pgraft prevents split-brain scenarios.**

**Split-Brain Prevention:**
pgraft provides 100% split-brain protection through the Raft consensus algorithm.

**Protection Mechanisms:**
1. **Quorum Requirement**: Leader needs majority votes (N/2 + 1)
2. **Term Monotonicity**: Higher term always wins elections
3. **Log Completeness**: Only up-to-date nodes can be elected leader
4. **Single Leader Per Term**: Mathematical guarantee from Raft algorithm

**Example Scenarios:**
\`\`\`sql
-- 3-node cluster with network partition
-- Nodes 1 and 2 can communicate, Node 3 is isolated

-- Node 1 (Leader): Can maintain leadership with 2/3 votes
SELECT pgraft_is_leader(); -- Returns true

-- Node 3 (Isolated): Cannot become leader (only 1/3 votes)
SELECT pgraft_is_leader(); -- Returns false

-- When network heals, Node 3 catches up automatically
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_cluster_status();
\`\`\`

**Mathematical Guarantees:**
- **Majority Requirement**: At least (N/2 + 1) nodes must agree
- **Election Safety**: Only one leader can exist per term
- **Leader Append-Only**: Leaders never overwrite or delete entries
- **State Machine Safety**: All nodes apply same commands in same order

**Network Partition Handling:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Monitor for network partitions
SELECT 
    node_id,
    last_heartbeat,
    CASE 
        WHEN last_heartbeat < NOW() - INTERVAL '5 seconds' THEN 'PARTITIONED'
        ELSE 'CONNECTED'
    END as network_status
FROM pgraft_node_health();
\`\`\`

**Recovery Procedures:**
- **Automatic Recovery**: Isolated nodes rejoin when connectivity restored
- **Log Synchronization**: All nodes synchronize to latest committed state
- **State Reconciliation**: Conflicting states resolved via Raft protocol
- **No Data Loss**: All committed operations are preserved`
            }
          ]
        },
        'Operations': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Monitoring',
              content: `**Monitoring pgraft clusters in production.**

**Key Metrics to Monitor:**
- **Cluster Health**: Overall cluster status and node availability
- **Leadership Status**: Current leader and election metrics
- **Replication Lag**: Data synchronization across nodes
- **Performance Metrics**: Throughput, latency, and resource usage

**Built-in Monitoring Functions:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Get comprehensive cluster status
SELECT * FROM pgraft_cluster_status();

-- Monitor node health
SELECT * FROM pgraft_node_health();

-- Check consensus metrics
SELECT * FROM pgraft_consensus_metrics();

-- Get performance statistics
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_performance_stats();
\`\`\`

**Prometheus Metrics:**
pgraft exposes metrics in Prometheus format for integration with monitoring systems.

\`\`\`
# Cluster metrics
pgraft_cluster_nodes_total{cluster="my-cluster"} 3
pgraft_cluster_leader_changes_total{cluster="my-cluster"} 2
pgraft_cluster_health_status{cluster="my-cluster"} 1

# Node metrics
pgraft_node_status{cluster="my-cluster",node="node1",role="leader"} 1
pgraft_node_uptime_seconds{cluster="my-cluster",node="node1"} 604800
pgraft_node_replication_lag_ms{cluster="my-cluster",node="node2"} 5

# Raft metrics
pgraft_raft_term{cluster="my-cluster",node="node1"} 5
pgraft_raft_committed_index{cluster="my-cluster",node="node1"} 10000
pgraft_raft_applied_index{cluster="my-cluster",node="node1"} 9995
\`\`\`

**Health Check Queries:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Create monitoring views
CREATE VIEW cluster_health AS
SELECT 
    node_id,
    is_leader,
    last_heartbeat,
    CASE 
        WHEN last_heartbeat < NOW() - INTERVAL '5 seconds' THEN 'STALE'
        ELSE 'ACTIVE'
    END as heartbeat_status
FROM pgraft_node_health();

-- Monitor for issues
SELECT * FROM cluster_health WHERE heartbeat_status = 'STALE';
\`\`\`

**Alerting Rules:**
\`\`\`yaml
groups:
- name: pgraft_alerts
  rules:
  - alert: ClusterDown
    expr: pgraft_cluster_health_status == 0
    for: 1m
    labels:
      severity: critical
    annotations:
      summary: "pgraft cluster is down"
      
  - alert: NodeDown
    expr: pgraft_node_status == 0
    for: 30s
    labels:
      severity: warning
    annotations:
      summary: "pgraft node {{ $labels.node }} is down"
      
  - alert: HighReplicationLag
    expr: pgraft_node_replication_lag_ms > 1000
    for: 2m
    labels:
      severity: warning
    annotations:
      summary: "High replication lag on node {{ $labels.node }}"
\`\`\``
            },
            {
              title: 'Best Practices',
              content: `**Production best practices for pgraft clusters.**

**Cluster Design:**
- **Node Count**: Use odd numbers (3, 5, 7) for optimal quorum
- **Network**: Ensure stable, low-latency connectivity between nodes
- **Hardware**: Use identical hardware for consistent performance
- **Placement**: Distribute nodes across availability zones

**Configuration Best Practices:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Recommended production settings
ALTER SYSTEM SET pgraft.heartbeat_interval = '100ms';
ALTER SYSTEM SET pgraft.election_timeout = '1000ms';
ALTER SYSTEM SET pgraft.max_log_entries = 10000;

-- For high-availability environments
ALTER SYSTEM SET pgraft.heartbeat_interval = '50ms';
ALTER SYSTEM SET pgraft.election_timeout = '500ms';

-- For unstable networks
ALTER SYSTEM SET pgraft.heartbeat_interval = '200ms';
ALTER SYSTEM SET pgraft.election_timeout = '2000ms';
\`\`\`

**Operational Guidelines:**
1. **Node Addition**: Always add nodes from the current leader
2. **Maintenance**: Use pgraft_pause_consensus() for planned maintenance
3. **Monitoring**: Set up comprehensive monitoring and alerting
4. **Backups**: Regular backups include pgraft consensus state
5. **Updates**: Test updates in staging environment first

**Maintenance Procedures:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Pre-maintenance checklist
SELECT * FROM pgraft_cluster_status();

-- Pause consensus for maintenance
SELECT pgraft_pause_consensus();

-- Perform maintenance operations
-- PostgreSQL updates, configuration changes, etc.

-- Resume consensus
SELECT pgraft_resume_consensus();

-- Verify cluster health
SELECT * FROM pgraft_node_health();
\`\`\`

**Security Considerations:**
- **Network Security**: Use TLS for inter-node communication
- **Authentication**: Implement proper authentication mechanisms
- **Access Control**: Restrict administrative access to cluster functions
- **Audit Logging**: Enable comprehensive audit logging

**Performance Optimization:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Monitor performance metrics
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_performance_stats();

-- Optimize for your workload
-- Adjust batch sizes, timeouts, and resource limits

-- Regular performance reviews
-- Monitor trends and optimize based on usage patterns
\`\`\`

**Disaster Recovery:**
- **Regular Backups**: Include pgraft state in backup procedures
- **Recovery Testing**: Test recovery procedures regularly
- **Documentation**: Maintain up-to-date operational documentation
- **Runbooks**: Create detailed runbooks for common scenarios`
            }
          ]
        },
        'Development': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Building from Source',
              content: `**Building pgraft from source code.**

**Prerequisites:**
- Go 1.21+
- C compiler (GCC 7.0+ or Clang 6.0+)
- PostgreSQL development headers
- Git
- Make

**Build Process:**
\`\`\`bash
# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/pgElephant/pgraft.git
cd pgraft

# Build the extension
make

# Install system-wide
sudo make install

# Verify installation
ls -la $(pg_config --pkglibdir)/pgraft.so
\`\`\`

**Development Build:**
\`\`\`bash
# Build with debug symbols
make DEBUG=1

# Build with custom PostgreSQL version
make PG_CONFIG=/usr/local/pgsql/bin/pg_config

# Build with specific Go version
make GO_VERSION=1.21
\`\`\`

**Build Configuration:**
\`\`\`bash
# Enable development features
make ENABLE_DEV_FEATURES=1

# Build with custom flags
make CFLAGS="-O2 -g" LDFLAGS="-Wl,--as-needed"

# Cross-compilation support
make GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64
\`\`\`

**Verification:**
\`\`\`bash
# Check build artifacts
ls -la pgraft.so
file pgraft.so

# Verify Go components
go version
go mod verify

# Test installation
make test-install
\`\`\``
            },
            {
              title: 'Testing',
              content: `**Testing pgraft functionality.**

**Unit Tests:**
\`\`\`bash
# Run Go unit tests
go test ./...

# Run with coverage
go test -cover ./...

# Run specific test packages
go test ./raft ./storage ./network
\`\`\`

**Integration Tests:**
\`\`\`bash
# Run PostgreSQL extension tests
make test

# Run cluster tests
make test-cluster

# Run performance tests
make test-performance
\`\`\`

**Test Configuration:**
\`\`\`bash
# Test with different PostgreSQL versions
make test PG_VERSION=15
make test PG_VERSION=16
make test PG_VERSION=17

# Test with different configurations
make test CONFIG=minimal
make test CONFIG=production
make test CONFIG=debug
\`\`\`

**Manual Testing:**
\`\`\`sql
-- Test basic functionality
CREATE EXTENSION pgraft;
SELECT pgraft_version();

-- Test cluster formation
SELECT pgraft_init();
SELECT pgraft_is_leader();

-- Test node addition
SELECT pgraft_add_node(2, '127.0.0.1', 7002);

-- Test cluster operations
SELECT * FROM pgraft_get_cluster_status();
\`\`\`

**Performance Testing:**
\`\`\`bash
# Run benchmark tests
go test -bench=. ./...

# Run stress tests
make test-stress

# Run long-running tests
make test-long
\`\`\``
            },
            {
              title: 'Contributing',
              content: `**Contributing to pgraft development.**

**Development Setup:**
\`\`\`bash
# Fork and clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/your-username/pgraft.git
cd pgraft

# Set up development environment
make dev-setup

# Install development dependencies
make dev-deps
\`\`\`

**Code Style:**
- **C Code**: Follow PostgreSQL C coding standards
- **Go Code**: Follow standard Go formatting (gofmt)
- **Comments**: Use C-style comments (/* */) for C code
- **Indentation**: Use tabs for C code, spaces for Go code

**Development Workflow:**
1. Create a feature branch
2. Make your changes
3. Add tests for new functionality
4. Run the test suite
5. Submit a pull request

**Testing Requirements:**
\`\`\`bash
# All tests must pass
make test

# Code must be properly formatted
make fmt

# No linting errors
make lint
\`\`\`

**Pull Request Process:**
- **Description**: Provide clear description of changes
- **Tests**: Include tests for new functionality
- **Documentation**: Update documentation if needed
- **Backwards Compatibility**: Consider impact on existing deployments

**Issue Reporting:**
When reporting issues, please include:
- PostgreSQL version
- Operating system
- Steps to reproduce
- Expected vs actual behavior
- Relevant log output

**Community Guidelines:**
- Be respectful and constructive
- Help others learn and grow
- Follow the code of conduct
- Contribute to discussions and reviews`
            }
          ]
        }
      },
        'Getting Started': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Installation',
              content: `pgraft is a PostgreSQL extension that implements Raft consensus for automatic leader election and failover.

1. **Prerequisites**:
   - PostgreSQL 16-18 with development headers
   - Go 1.21+
   - Build tools (GCC/Clang, Make, pkg-config)

2. **Clone and build**:
   \`\`\`bash
   git clone https://github.com/pgElephant/pgraft.git
   cd pgraft
   make && sudo make install
   \`\`\`

3. **Configure PostgreSQL**:
   \`\`\`conf
   # Add to postgresql.conf
   shared_preload_libraries = 'pgraft'
   \`\`\`

4. **Create extension**:
   \`\`\`sql
   CREATE EXTENSION pgraft;
   \`\`\``
            },
            {
              title: 'Quick Start',
              content: `**Initialize cluster:**
\`\`\`sql
SELECT pgraft_init_cluster('my-cluster');
\`\`\`

**Add nodes:**
\`\`\`sql
SELECT pgraft_add_member('my-cluster', 'node1', 'host=192.168.1.10 port=5432');
SELECT pgraft_add_member('my-cluster', 'node2', 'host=192.168.1.11 port=5432');
SELECT pgraft_add_member('my-cluster', 'node3', 'host=192.168.1.12 port=5432');
\`\`\`

**Check status:**
\`\`\`sql
SELECT * FROM pgraft_cluster_status('my-cluster');
SELECT * FROM pgraft_leader('my-cluster');
\`\`\``
            }
          ]
        },
        'Installation': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Detailed Installation Guide',
              content: `Complete installation guide for pgraft PostgreSQL Raft extension. Build from source, configure PostgreSQL, and enable the extension.

**Prerequisites:**
- PostgreSQL 16-18 with development headers
- Go 1.21+ for Raft implementation
- Build tools: GCC/Clang, Make, pkg-config

**Installation Steps:**
1. Clone repository and build
2. Install extension files
3. Configure PostgreSQL
4. Create and verify extension

Visit the full Installation page for detailed instructions.`
            }
          ]
        },
        'Configuration': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Configuration Guide',
              content: `Configure pgraft extension and PostgreSQL for optimal Raft consensus performance.

**PostgreSQL Configuration:**
- shared_preload_libraries = 'pgraft'
- Memory and connection settings
- WAL configuration for replication

**pgraft Parameters:**
- heartbeat_interval: Leader heartbeat frequency
- election_timeout: Timeout for leader election
- snapshot_threshold: Log entries before snapshot

Visit the full Configuration page for complete settings.`
            }
          ]
        },
        'SQL Functions': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'SQL Function Reference',
              content: `Complete reference for all pgraft SQL functions and procedures.

**Cluster Management:**
- pgraft_init_cluster(): Initialize new cluster
- pgraft_add_member(): Add node to cluster
- pgraft_remove_member(): Remove node from cluster

**Leadership Functions:**
- pgraft_leader(): Get current leader
- pgraft_is_leader(): Check if node is leader
- pgraft_force_election(): Trigger election

Visit the full SQL Functions page for complete reference.`
            }
          ]
        },
        'Raft Protocol': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Raft Consensus Protocol',
              content: `Understanding Raft consensus protocol implementation in pgraft PostgreSQL extension.

**Raft Overview:**
- Distributed consensus algorithm
- Leader election and log replication
- Safety properties and guarantees

**Node States:**
- Leader: Handles requests and replicates logs
- Follower: Receives logs from leader
- Candidate: Requests votes during election

Visit the full Raft Protocol page for detailed explanation.`
            }
          ]
        },
        'Cluster Management': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Cluster Management Guide',
              content: `Managing PostgreSQL clusters with pgraft Raft consensus for high availability and automatic failover.

**Cluster Lifecycle:**
1. Cluster creation and initialization
2. Node addition and removal
3. Health monitoring and maintenance

**Failover Management:**
- Automatic failover process
- Manual failover procedures
- Recovery and troubleshooting

Visit the full Cluster Management page for operational procedures.`
            }
          ]
        },
        'Performance Tuning': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Performance Optimization',
              content: `Optimize pgraft PostgreSQL Raft extension for maximum performance and throughput.

**Configuration Strategies:**
- High throughput configuration
- Low latency configuration
- Balanced performance settings

**Monitoring and Tuning:**
- Key performance metrics
- Network optimization
- PostgreSQL tuning

Visit the full Performance Tuning page for optimization strategies.`
            }
          ]
        },
        'Troubleshooting': {
          sections: [
            {
              title: 'Troubleshooting Guide',
              content: `Resolve common issues and errors with pgraft PostgreSQL Raft extension.

**Common Issues:**
- Extension won't load
- Cluster split-brain scenarios
- High replication latency
- Network connectivity problems

**Diagnostic Commands:**
- Health check procedures
- Configuration validation
- Performance analysis

Visit the full Troubleshooting page for solutions and recovery procedures.`
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
